nodecode-channel-nextcloud-talk is a rewrite of smilinTux's smilinTux/hermes-nextcloud-talk:

  https://github.com/smilinTux/hermes-nextcloud-talk
  commit 03f27203fd983d4714891302c49f06b404c4b02f
  MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 smilinTux (LICENSE)

The rewritten plugin is the repository root of that commit (__init__.py,
plugin.yaml, bot/ and user/). What was carried over: the user-account
connector — an app password for a real Nextcloud account, the OCS room list
(/ocs/v2.php/apps/spreed/api/v4/room), the chat long poll
(/ocs/v2.php/apps/spreed/api/v1/chat/<token> with lookIntoFuture,
lastKnownMessageId and a timeout, 304 for a quiet room), posting with
replyTo, the reaction endpoint, the typing signal, the 32000-character
message cap, the `mention` admission rule with its rich
`{mention-userN}` placeholders resolved and Talk's quoted `@"Display Name"`
form, the same rule read outside code spans, and the leading self-mention
stripped so a slash command arrives as one. The room description handed to
the model as room rules, and the backlog of unanswered messages handed over
with the next answered one, are the original's too, as is the room-type
split between one-to-one (types 1 and 6) and group rooms.

What was left out, and why: bot mode, the webhook connector with an inbound
HTTP listener, HMAC-SHA256 on both directions, the nonce-replay cache and
the request-body cap, because a cell reaches Talk over the account's own
REST API and needs no port open and no secret shared with the server;
federation-invite auto-accept and its inviter allowlist, which a cell
replaces by the account being invited normally; the `watch` room mode and
its periodic digest, and the multi-agent chain limit and agent allowlist,
which are loop-avoidance bookkeeping of the original's runtime rather than
anything Talk asks for; and the update/`/sethome` commands and the
standalone sender, whose answers the section, `/settings` and the lane
state give here. LICENSE is the repository's LICENSE, unchanged.
